## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React re-renders a component when its props or state change. The comparison is shallow (`===`), so **two objects with identical contents are not equal** — each render creates a new reference.

```jsx
// Causes re-render every time Parent renders
<Child style={{ color: 'red' }} />         // new object each render
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />    // new function each render
```

Primitives (`string`, `number`, `boolean`) are compared by value, so they're fine inline. Objects, arrays, and functions are compared by reference — they must be stabilized.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

**`React.memo`** — wraps a component; skips re-render if props haven't changed (shallow compare). Only useful if the parent re-renders frequently and the child is expensive.

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(({ label, onClick }) => <button onClick={onClick}>{label}</button>);
```

**`useMemo`** — memoizes a computed value. Use when the computation is expensive, not just to "avoid objects."

```jsx
const filtered = useMemo(() => list.filter(expensive), [list]);
```

**`useCallback`** — memoizes a function reference. Use when passing a callback to a memoized child (otherwise `React.memo` is defeated by a new function reference each render).

```jsx
const handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(id), [id]);
```

**Rule of thumb:** `React.memo` is pointless without stable prop references. `useMemo`/`useCallback` are pointless without `React.memo` on the child (or a dependency array somewhere that needs stability).

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

A context value that is a new object on every render will re-render **every consumer**, even if the data is logically identical.

```jsx
// BAD — new object every render
<UserContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
```

```jsx
// GOOD — stable reference
const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user]);
<UserContext.Provider value={value}>
```

Also: **avoid putting unrelated state in the same context.** A component that only needs `theme` will re-render when `user` changes if they share a context.

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — `List` re-renders on every `Page` render, even though its props don't change:

```jsx
function Page() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Increment</button>
      {/* New array and new function reference every render */}
      <List
        items={['a', 'b', 'c']}
        onSelect={(item) => console.log(item)}
      />
    </>
  );
}

function List({ items, onSelect }) {
  console.log('List rendered');
  return items.map(i => <div key={i} onClick={() => onSelect(i)}>{i}</div>);
}
```

**After** — `List` only re-renders when `items` or `onSelect` actually change:

```jsx
const ITEMS = ['a', 'b', 'c']; // stable: defined outside component

function Page() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const handleSelect = useCallback((item) => console.log(item), []); // stable reference

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Increment</button>
      <List items={ITEMS} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

const List = React.memo(function List({ items, onSelect }) {
  console.log('List rendered');
  return items.map(i => <div key={i} onClick={() => onSelect(i)}>{i}</div>);
});
```

**What changed:**
- `ITEMS` moved outside the component (constant reference)
- `handleSelect` wrapped in `useCallback` (stable function reference)
- `List` wrapped in `React.memo` (skips render when props are shallowly equal)

Without all three pieces, the optimization doesn't work — `React.memo` needs stable props, and `useCallback` is wasteful without `React.memo` consuming it.
